  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 225 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and comfortable conditions continue tonight with \nunseasonably warm weather and chances for showers and storms \nreturning late week and into the weekend.\n\n1028mb high pressure remains stationed over the Mid-Atlantic with \nmostly clear skies noted over the region. An unseasonably strong \n(600dm) upper ridge is noted just to our SW. A few cumulus clouds \nhave developed across the eastern third of the area, primarily near \nthe Ches Bay and coast. Temperatures have climbed to around seasonal \nnorms this afternoon with most locations in the upper 70s to low \n80s. A few spots in the Piedmont may make it into the mid 80s over \nthe next hour or two. \n\nHigh pressure slowly moves offshore this evening and tonight with \nlow level flow becoming S or SE. It looks like we will get one more \npleasant night with lows in the upper 50s to around 60 degrees. \nAbove average temps return to the region Thursday with highs back \ninto the upper 80s and low 90s inland and mid to upper 80s near the \ncoast. A front will drop slowly southward from the Ohio Valley \ntoward the region with a few showers or storms possible across far \nNW portions of the CWA (Louisa and Fluvanna, primarily) during the \nafternoon and early evening. Not expecting much coverage or rainfall \nfrom any convection that manages to form on Thursday. A few showers \nmay linger across the far northern portion of the area into Thursday \nevening with overnight lows in the mid 60s to low 70s. \n\nThe front will cross the area Friday morning as strong high pressure \nover the Great Lakes builds southward. Some pre or post-frontal \nshowers are possible on Friday, mainly over the SW half of the area \nbut QPF continues to be rather light (0.1 to 0.25\"). High temps \nrange from the mid 80s N to the low 90s S. Mostly dry and somewhat \ncooler on Saturday behind the front with highs in the low to mid \n80s. The front likely lifts back to the north Sunday into early next \nweek with increasing deep moisture and chances for diurnal \nshowers and storms.",
